"New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2308.09031)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{khesin2023newpropertiesintrinsicinformation, title={New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy}, author={Andrey Boris Khesin and Andrew Tung and Karthik Vedula}, year={2023}, eprint={2308.09031}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.IT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09031}, }\nSubmitted 08 Oct 2023\n\n# New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy\n\nAB\n\nAndrey Boris Khesin\n\nAT\n\nAndrew Tung\n\nKV\n\nKarthik Vedula\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe secret-key rate measures the rate at which Alice and Bob can extract secret bits from sampling a joint probability distribution, unknown to an eavesdropper Eve. The secret-key rate has been bounded above by the intrinsic information and reduced intrinsic information. However, we prove that the reduced intrinsic information is 0 if and only if the intrinsic information is 0. This result implies that at least one of the following two conjectures is false: bound secrecy exists, or the reduced intrinsic information equals the secret-key rate. We give an explicit construction of an information-erasing binarization for a candidate for bound secrecy. We then introduce some approaches for proving the existence of bound secrecy, such as reducing the channel space, linearly transforming Bob's map, and perturbing a channel for Eve.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{khesin2023newpropertiesintrinsicinformation, title={New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy}, author={Andrey Boris Khesin and Andrew Tung and Karthik Vedula}, year={2023}, eprint={2308.09031}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.IT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09031}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScience Cast (https://phys.sciencecast.org/digest_issues/2830)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: last week; New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy\n\n2308.09031\n\n##### 9.New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy\n\n2308.09031\n\nAuthors:Andrey Khesin, Andrew Tung, Karthik Vedula\n\n## Abstract & Audio\n\nAbstract: The secret-key rate measures the rate at which Alice and Bob can extract secret bits from sampling a joint probability distribution, unknown to an eavesdropper Eve. The secret-key rate has been bounded above by the intrinsic information and reduced intrinsic information. However, we prove that the reduced intrinsic information is 0 if and only if the intrinsic information is 0. This result implies that at least one of the following two conjectures is false: bound secrecy exists, or the reduced intrinsic information equals the secret-key rate. We give an explicit construction of an information-erasing binarization for a candidate for bound secrecy. We then introduce some approaches for proving the existence of bound secrecy, such as reducing the channel space, linearly transforming Bob's map, and perturbing a channel for Eve.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScience Cast (https://phys.sciencecast.org/digest_issues/3326)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: last week; Existence of balanced functions that are not derivative of bent functions\n\n2309.04244 ... Abstract: It is disproved the Tokareva's conjecture that any balanced boolean function of appropriate degree is a derivative of some bent function.\n\n##### 7.Existence of balanced functions that are not derivative of bent functions\n\n2309.04244\n\nAuthors:Vladimir N. Potapov\n\n## Abstract & Audio\n\nAbstract: It is disproved the Tokareva's conjecture that any balanced boolean function of appropriate degree is a derivative of some bent function. This result is based on new upper bounds for the numbers of bent and plateaued functions.\n\n##### 8.Modulation and Estimation with a Helper\n\n2309.04277\n\nAuthors:Anatoly Khina, Neri Merhav\n\n## Abstract & Audio\n\nAbstract: The problem of transmitting a parameter value over an additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN) channel is considered, where, in addition to the transmitter and the receiver, there is a helper that observes the noise non-causally and provides a description of limited rate $R_\\mathrm{h}$ to the transmitter and/or the receiver. We derive upper and lower bounds on the optimal achievable $\\alpha$-th moment of the estimation error and show that they coincide for small values of $\\alpha$ and for low SNR values. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/replicate/2308.09031)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{khesin2023newpropertiesintrinsicinformation, title={New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy}, author={Andrey Boris Khesin and Andrew Tung and Karthik Vedula}, year={2023}, eprint={2308.09031}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.IT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09031}, }\n\n2 views\n\nv2\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{khesin2023newpropertiesintrinsicinformation, title={New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy}, author={Andrey Boris Khesin and Andrew Tung and Karthik Vedula}, year={2023}, eprint={2308.09031}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.IT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09031}, }\n\n## Related papers\n\n# New Properties of Intrinsic Information and Their Relation to Bound Secrecy--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fsearch%2F%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D&query=Nambi%2C+M&searchtype=author)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   7. arXiv:2309.15424  [pdf, ps, other] ... Abstract: In this paper, we prove the conjectures of Gharakhloo and Welker (2023) that the positive matching decomposition number (pmd) of a $3$-uniform hypergraph is bounded from above by a polynomial of degree $2$ in terms of the number of vertices.\nSubmitted 27 August, 2026; v1 submitted 9 June, 2024; originally announced June 2024.\n\nComments: comments are welcome, to appear in Journal of Commutative Algebra\n\nMSC Class: 05E40; 13F65; 13F70\n\n  7. arXiv:2309.15424  [pdf, ps, other] \n\nmath.AC\n\ndoi 10.1142/S0218196725500390\n\nOn Positive Matching Decomposition Conjectures of Hypergraphs\n\nAuthors: Marie Amalore Nambi, Neeraj Kumar\n\nAbstract: In this paper, we prove the conjectures of Gharakhloo and Welker (2023) that the positive matching decomposition number (pmd) of a $3$-uniform hypergraph is bounded from above by a polynomial of degree $2$ in terms of the number of vertices. Moreover, we derive a lower bound for pmd specifically for complete $3$-uniform hypergraphs. Additionally, we obtain an upper bound for pmd of $r$-uniform hyp… ▽ More In this paper, we prove the conjectures of Gharakhloo and Welker (2023) that the positive matching decomposition number (pmd) of a $3$-uniform hypergraph is bounded from above by a polynomial of degree $2$ in terms of the number of vertices. Moreover, we derive a lower bound for pmd specifically for complete $3$-uniform hypergraphs. Additionally, we obtain an upper bound for pmd of $r$-uniform hypergraphs. For a $r$-uniform hypergraphs $H=(V,E)$ such that $\\lvert e_i\\cap e_j\\rvert \\leq 1$ for all $e_i,e_j \\in E$, we give a characterization of positive matching in terms of strong alternate closed walks. For a specific class of hypergraphs, we classify the radical and complete intersection Lovász$-$Saks$-$Schrijver ideals. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 9 October, 2025; v1 submitted 27 September, 2023; originally announced September 2023.\n\nComments: To appear in International Journal of Algebra and Computation\n\nMSC Class: Primary 05C70; 13F65; 13F70; 13C40. Secondary 05C75; 05E40\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScience Cast (https://www.sciencecast.org/digest_issues/3326)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Existence of balanced functions that are not derivative of bent functions\n\n2309.04244\n\n2309.04221\n\nAuthors:Thach V. Bui, Jonathan Scarlett\n\n## Abstract & Audio\n\nAbstract: In this paper, we introduce a variation of the group testing problem capturing the idea that a positive test requires a combination of multiple ``types'' of item. Specifically, we assume that there are multiple disjoint \\emph{semi-defective sets}, and a test is positive if and only if it contains at least one item from each of these sets. The goal is to reliably identify all of the semi-defective sets using as few tests as possible, and we refer to this problem as \\textit{Concomitant Group Testing} (ConcGT). We derive a variety of algorithms for this task, focusing primarily on the case that there are two semi-defective sets. Our algorithms are distinguished by (i) whether they are deterministic (zero-error) or randomized (small-error), and (ii) whether they are non-adaptive, fully adaptive, or have limited adaptivity (e.g., 2 or 3 stages). Both our deterministic adaptive algorithm and our randomized algorithms (non-adaptive or limited adaptivity) are order-optimal in broad scaling regimes of interest, and improve significantly over baseline results that are based on solving a more general problem as an intermediate step (e.g., hypergraph learning).\n\n##### 7.Existence of balanced functions that are not derivative of bent functions\n\n2309.04244\n\nAuthors:Vladimir N. Potapov\n\n----------